<div class="section" id="key-takeaways">
    <h2><span class="section-num">6</span>Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>
    <p class="section-objective">Synthesize findings into actionable insights for investors, hosts, and policymakers.</p>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">1</span>
        <span class="tk-title">Amsterdam and London are the most accessible markets for new hosts.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Amsterdam has the lowest professional host concentration (6.8%) and the most compressed pricing (CV = 0.83), meaning individual hosts face less competition from large operators and pricing is more predictable. London's moderate professional share (32.2%) combined with high listing volume (16,674) creates a large but still contestable market.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">2</span>
        <span class="tk-title">Bangkok and Barcelona are professional-host markets with distinct risk profiles.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Over 58-60% of listings in these cities are managed by hosts with 5+ properties, indicating professional dominance. Bangkok's extremely high availability (252.5 mean days) and low review rate (0.96/month) suggest oversupply risk — many listings sit vacant. Barcelona's high minimum-night requirement (38.5% over 30 nights) signals regulatory pressure that could limit short-term rental viability.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">3</span>
        <span class="tk-title">Rome offers the strongest guest demand signals.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Rome leads in reviews per month (1.77), review recency (66.3% within 12 months), and ever-reviewed rate (85.4%), indicating consistent, active guest engagement. Its median price ($131) is among the lowest, suggesting potential for yield optimization — demand is strong but pricing has not been pushed to the levels seen in Amsterdam or London.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">4</span>
        <span class="tk-title">Regulatory environments are visible in availability and minimum-night data.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Amsterdam (39.6% zero availability) and Paris (36.0%) show the clearest regulatory signatures, with large shares of listings blocked from booking. Barcelona's 38.5% of listings requiring 30+ night minimums is another regulatory artifact. These markets require careful due diligence — what appears as supply may not actually be bookable for short-term stays.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">5</span>
        <span class="tk-title">Entire-home dominance varies widely and signals commercialization level.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Paris (88.2% entire homes) and Amsterdam (82.8%) have the highest entire-home shares, while Barcelona (60.2%) and London (64.7%) have more diverse room-type mixes. A high entire-home share combined with high professional host concentration (as in Bangkok) is a strong commercialization signal, whereas a high entire-home share with low professional concentration (Amsterdam) suggests a market of individual hosts renting out their primary residences.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">6</span>
        <span class="tk-title">Pricing data gaps are a significant analytical limitation.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Paris (13,927 listings) and Sydney (3,013 listings) — representing 34% of the dataset — have zero price data, making cross-market pricing comparisons incomplete. Bangkok's pricing appears anomalously high, likely due to currency normalization issues. Any pricing-based investment decision should verify currency conversion methodology and seek supplemental data sources before proceeding.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">7</span>
        <span class="tk-title">Sydney occupies a middle ground worth monitoring.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Sydney shows moderate professional host share (42.3%), strong review recency (62.2% within 12 months), and reasonable availability (176.2 mean days). Despite lacking price data, its demand signals and moderate commercialization suggest a balanced market. The 16.6% of listings with 30+ night minimums may reflect evolving regulatory responses to short-term rental growth.</div>
    </div>
</div>

