   <th>City</th>", "+                    <th>Professional Host Share</th>", "+                    <th>Entire Home/Apt Share</th>", "+                    <th>Mean Listings/Host</th>", "+                    <th>Median Listings/Host</th>", "+                    <th>Max Listings (Single Host)</th>", "+                </tr>", "+            </thead>", "+            <tbody>", "+                {''.join(f'<tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td><td>{r[3]}</td><td>{r[4]}</td><td>{r[5]}</td></tr>' for r in table_supply_rows)}", "+            </tbody>", "+        </table>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="highlight-box">", "+        <strong>Commercialization Gradient:</strong> Bangkok (60.4%) and Barcelona (58.3%) have the highest professional host concentrations, indicating supply is dominated by operators running multiple listings. In contrast, Amsterdam (6.8%) is overwhelmingly individual-host-driven, consistent with its strict short-term rental regulations. Barcelona's high professional share combined with its high minimum-night requirements (38.5% over 30 nights) suggests many listings operate as de facto long-term rentals under professional management.", "+    </div>", "+</div>", "+", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<!-- SECTION 4: GUEST DEMAND & REVIEW ACTIVITY                    -->", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<div class="section" id="guest-demand">", "+    <h2><span class="section-num">4</span>Guest Demand & Review Activity</h2>", "+    <p class="section-objective">Use review metrics as demand proxies to compare guest engagement across cities and room types, and identify recency patterns.</p>", "+", "+    <div class="kpi-grid">", "+        <div class="kpi-card">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">1.77</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Highest Avg Reviews/Mo (Rome)</div>", "+        </div>", "+        <div class="kpi-card alt">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">0.96</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Lowest Avg Reviews/Mo (Bangkok)</div>", "+        </div>", "+        <div class="kpi-card accent">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">66.3%</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Highest 12-Mo Review Recency (Rome)</div>", "+        </div>", "+        <div class="kpi-card">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">85.4%</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Highest Ever-Reviewed Rate (Rome)</div>", "+        </div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="chart-container">", "+        <canvas id="chartReviewsPerMonth"></canvas>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="table-wrap">", "+        <table>", "+            <thead>", "+                <tr>", "+                    <th>City</th>", "+                    <th>Avg Reviews/Month</th>", "+                    <th>Avg Total Reviews</th>", "+                    <th>Avg LTM Reviews</th>", "+                    <th>% Ever Reviewed</th>", "+                    <th>% Reviewed (Last 12 Mo)</th>", "+                </tr>", "+            </thead>", "+            <tbody>", "+                {''.join(f'<tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td><td>{r[3]}</td><td>{r[4]}</td><td>{r[5]}</td></tr>' for r in table_review_rows)}", "+            </tbody>", "+        </table>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="highlight-box">", "+        <strong>Demand Signal:</strong> Rome leads in review frequency (1.77/month) and review recency (66.3% reviewed within 12 months), indicating strong and recent guest demand. Bangkok has the lowest review rate (0.96/month) despite high listing volume, and only 64.4% of Bangkok listings have ever been reviewed — potentially signaling oversupply or lower occupancy rates. Note: the 12-month recency metric uses a reference date of the scrape date (2026-03-29); the most recent review in the data is from September 2025, which may affect recency calculations.", "+    </div>", "+</div>", "+", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<!-- SECTION 5: AVAILABILITY & BOOKING CONSTRAINTS                -->", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<div class="section" id="availability">", "+    <h2><span class="section-num">5</span>Availability & Booking Constraints</h2>", "+    <p class="section-objective">Examine availability and minimum-night distributions to understand listing accessibility and potential regulatory impacts.</p>", "+", "+    <div class="kpi-grid">", "+        <div class="kpi-card">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">252.5</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Highest Mean Availability (Bangkok)</div>", "+        </div>", "+        <div class="kpi-card alt">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">39.6%</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Highest Zero-Avail Rate (Amsterdam)</div>", "+        </div>", "+        <div class="kpi-card accent">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">38.5%</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Barcelona 30+ Night Min</div>", "+        </div>", "+        <div class="kpi-card">", "+            <div class="kpi-value">365</div>", "+            <div class="kpi-label">Max Minimum Nights (Multiple Cities)</div>", "+        </div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="chart-container">", "+        <canvas id="chartAvailability"></canvas>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin: 24px 0 12px; font-size:1.15em;">Zero-Availability Listings by City</h3>", "+", "+    <div class="chart-container">", "+        <canvas id="chartZeroAvail"></canvas>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="table-wrap">", "+        <table>", "+            <thead>", "+                <tr>", "+                    <th>City</th>", "+                    <th>Mean Availability (days)</th>", "+                    <th>Median Availability (days)</th>", "+                    <th>% Zero Availability</th>", "+                    <th>Mean Min. Nights</th>", "+                    <th>Median Min. Nights</th>", "+                    <th>% Min. Nights > 30</th>", "+                </tr>", "+            </thead>", "+            <tbody>", "+                {''.join(f'<tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td><td>{r[3]}</td><td>{r[4]}</td><td>{r[5]}</td><td>{r[6]}</td></tr>' for r in table_avail_rows)}", "+            </tbody>", "+        </table>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="highlight-box">", "+        <strong>Regulatory Footprint:</strong> Amsterdam (39.6% zero availability, mean 92.5 days) and Paris (36.0% zero availability, mean 125.2 days) show the strongest regulatory impacts — many listings appear blocked from booking, likely due to enforcement of short-term rental caps. Barcelona's 38.5% of listings requiring 30+ night minimums is a clear signal of regulatory compliance measures (or circumvention), pushing listings into the long-term rental category.", "+    </div>", "+</div>", "+", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<!-- SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS                                     -->", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<div class="section" id="key-takeaways">", "+    <h2><span class="section-num">6</span>Key Takeaways & Strategic Implications</h2>", "+    <p class="section-objective">Synthesize findings into actionable insights for investors, hosts, and policymakers.</p>", "+", "+    <div class="takeaway">", "+        <span class="tk-num">1</span>", "+        <span class="tk-title">Amsterdam and London are the most accessible markets for new hosts.</span>", "+        <div class="tk-body">Amsterdam has the lowest professional host concentration (6.8%) and the most compressed pricing (CV = 0.83), meaning individual hosts face less competition from large operators and pricing is more predictable. London's moderate professional share (32.2%) combined with high listing volume (16,674) creates a large but still contestable market.</div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="takeaway">", "+        <span class="tk-num">2</span>", "+        <span class="tk-title">Bangkok and Barcelona are professional-host markets with distinct risk profiles.</span>", "+        <div class="tk-body">Over 58-60% of listings in these cities are managed by hosts with 5+ properties, indicating professional dominance. Bangkok's extremely high availability (252.5 mean days) and low review rate (0.96/month) suggest oversupply risk — many listings sit vacant. Barcelona's high minimum-night requirement (38.5% over 30 nights) signals regulatory pressure that could limit short-term rental viability.</div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="takeaway">", "+        <span class="tk-num">3</span>", "+        <span class="tk-title">Rome offers the strongest guest demand signals.</span>", "+        <div class="tk-body">Rome leads in reviews per month (1.77), review recency (66.3% within 12 months), and ever-reviewed rate (85.4%), indicating consistent, active guest engagement. Its median price ($131) is among the lowest, suggesting potential for yield optimization — demand is strong but pricing has not been pushed to the levels seen in Amsterdam or London.</div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="takeaway">", "+        <span class="tk-num">4</span>", "+        <span class="tk-title">Regulatory environments are visible in availability and minimum-night data.</span>", "+        <div class="tk-body">Amsterdam (39.6% zero availability) and Paris (36.0%) show the clearest regulatory signatures, with large shares of listings blocked from booking. Barcelona's 38.5% of listings requiring 30+ night minimums is another regulatory artifact. These markets require careful due diligence — what appears as supply may not actually be bookable for short-term stays.</div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="takeaway">", "+        <span class="tk-num">5</span>", "+        <span class="tk-title">Entire-home dominance varies widely and signals commercialization level.</span>", "+        <div class="tk-body">Paris (88.2% entire homes) and Amsterdam (82.8%) have the highest entire-home shares, while Barcelona (60.2%) and London (64.7%) have more diverse room-type mixes. A high entire-home share combined with high professional host concentration (as in Bangkok) is a strong commercialization signal, whereas a high entire-home share with low professional concentration (Amsterdam) suggests a market of individual hosts renting out their primary residences.</div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="takeaway">", "+        <span class="tk-num">6</span>", "+        <span class="tk-title">Pricing data gaps are a significant analytical limitation.</span>", "+        <div class="tk-body">Paris (13,927 listings) and Sydney (3,013 listings) — representing 34% of the dataset — have zero price data, making cross-market pricing comparisons incomplete. Bangkok's pricing appears anomalously high, likely due to currency normalization issues. Any pricing-based investment decision should verify currency conversion methodology and seek supplemental data sources before proceeding.</div>", "+    </div>", "+", "+    <div class="takeaway">", "+        <span class="tk-num">7</span>", "+        <span class="tk-title">Sydney occupies a middle ground worth monitoring.</span>", "+        <div class="tk-body">Sydney shows moderate professional host share (42.3%), strong review recency (62.2% within 12 months), and reasonable availability (176.2 mean days). Despite lacking price data, its demand signals and moderate commercialization suggest a balanced market. The 16.6% of listings with 30+ night minimums may reflect evolving regulatory responses to short-term rental growth.</div>", "+    </div>", "+</div>", "+", "+<!-- FOOTER -->", "+<div class="report-footer">", "+    <p>Data Source: Inside Airbnb (CC BY 4.0) | Analysis based on data.csv with {len(df):,} listings across 7 cities</p>", "+    <p>Report generated on {datetime.now().strftime('%B %d, %Y')} | All figures derived directly from source data — no imputation or fabrication</p>", "+</div>", "+", "+</div>", "+", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<!-- CHART.JS SCRIPTS                                              -->", "+<!-- ============================================================ -->", "+<script>", "+// Color palette", "+const cityColors = {{", "+    'London': 'rgba(52, 152, 219, 0.8)',", "+    'Paris': 'rgba(155, 89, 182, 0.8)',", "+    'Rome': 'rgba(46, 204, 113, 0.8)',", "+    'Bangkok': 'rgba(243, 156, 18, 0.8)',", "+    'Barcelona': 'rgba(231, 76, 60, 0.8)',", "+    'Sydney': 'rgba(26, 188, 156, 0.8)',", "+    'Amsterdam': 'rgba(52, 73, 94, 0.8)'", "+}};", "+const cityBorders = {{", "+    'London': 'rgba(52, 152, 219, 1)',", "+    'Paris': 'rgba(155, 89, 182, 1)',", "+    'Rome': 'rgba(46, 204, 113, 1)',", "+    'Bangkok': 'rgba(243, 156, 18, 1)',", "+    'Barcelona': 'rgba(231, 76, 60, 1)',", "+    'Sydney': 'rgba(26, 188, 156, 1)',", "+    'Amsterdam': 'rgba(52, 73, 94, 1)'", "+}};", "+", "+// Common chart options", "+const baseOptions = {{", "+    responsive: true,", "+    maintainAspectRatio: true,", "+    plugins: {{", "+        legend: {{ position: 'top' }},", "+        tooltip: {{ mode: 'index', intersect: false }}", "+    }}", "+}};", "+", "+// Chart 1: Listings per city", "+new Chart(document.getElementById('chartListingsPerCity'), {{", "+    type: 'bar',", "+    data: {{", "+        labels: {json.dumps(chart1_labels)},", "+        datasets: [{{", "+            label: 'Total Listings',", "+            data: {json.dumps(chart1_data)},", "+            backgroundColor: {json.dumps([cityColors.get(c, 'rgba(100,100,100,0.8)') for c in chart1_labels])},", "+            borderColor: {json.dumps([cityBorders.get(c, 'rgba(100,100,100,1)') for c in chart1_labels])},", "+            borderWidth: 1", "+        }}]", "+    }},", "+    options: {{...baseOptions, indexAxis: 'y',", "+  