    </table></div>
    <h3 class="section-sub">Price Dispersion by City</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Median</th><th>Mean</th><th>25th Pctl</th><th>75th Pctl</th><th>IQR</th><th>CV (Std/Mean)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>""" + render_rows(table_price_rows) + """</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="highlight-box"><strong>Dispersion Insight:</strong> London exhibits the highest coefficient of variation (2.55), indicating extreme price spread &mdash; from budget shared rooms to luxury listings. Amsterdam has the lowest CV (0.83), suggesting a more compressed and predictable pricing range, which may reflect stronger regulatory uniformity.</div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="section" id="supply-structure">
    <h2><span class="section-num">3</span>Supply Structure &amp; Host Concentration</h2>
    <p class="section-objective">Analyze room-type distribution, host concentration (professional vs. individual), and identify cities where supply is most commercialized.</p>
    <div class="kpi-grid">
        <div class="kpi-card"><div class="kpi-value">60.4%</div><div class="kpi-label">Highest Pro Host Share (Bangkok)</div></div>
        <div class="kpi-card alt"><div class="kpi-value">6.8%</div><div class="kpi-label">Lowest Pro Host Share (Amsterdam)</div></div>
