    </div>
    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">3</span><span class="tk-title">Rome offers the strongest guest demand signals.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Rome leads in reviews per month (1.77), review recency (66.3% within 12 months), and ever-reviewed rate (85.4%), indicating consistent, active guest engagement. Its median price ($131) is among the lowest, suggesting potential for yield optimization &mdash; demand is strong but pricing has not been pushed to the levels seen in Amsterdam or London.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">4</span><span class="tk-title">Regulatory environments are visible in availability and minimum-night data.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Amsterdam (39.6% zero availability) and Paris (36.0%) show the clearest regulatory signatures, with large shares of listings blocked from booking. Barcelona's 38.5% of listings requiring 30+ night minimums is another regulatory artifact. These markets require careful due diligence &mdash; what appears as supply may not actually be bookable for short-term stays.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">5</span><span class="tk-title">Entire-home dominance varies widely and signals commercialization level.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Paris (88.2% entire homes) and Amsterdam (82.8%) have the highest entire-home shares, while Barcelona (60.2%) and London (64.7%) have more diverse room-type mixes. A high entire-home share combined with high professional host concentration (as in Bangkok) is a strong commercialization signal, whereas a high entire-home share with low professional concentration (Amsterdam) suggests a market of individual hosts renting out their primary residences.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">6</span><span class="tk-title">Pricing data gaps are a significant analytical limitation.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Paris (13,927 listings) and Sydney (3,013 listings) &mdash; representing 34% of the dataset &mdash; have zero price data, making cross-market pricing comparisons incomplete. Bangkok's pricing appears anomalously high, likely due to currency normalization issues. Any pricing-based investment decision should verify currency conversion methodology and seek supplemental data sources before proceeding.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway">
        <span class="tk-num">7</span><span class="tk-title">Sydney occupies a middle ground worth monitoring.</span>
        <div class="tk-body">Sydney shows moderate professional host share (42.3%), strong review recency (62.2% within 12 months), and reasonable availability (176.2 mean days). Despite lacking price data, its demand signals and moderate commercialization suggest a balanced market. The 16.6% of listings with 30+ night minimums may reflect evolving regulatory responses to short-term rental growth.</div>
